
╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║        JAVA SETUP & FIRST PROGRAM — QUICK NOTES          ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

1. INSTALL JAVA
   winget install Microsoft.OpenJDK.21

2. SET ENVIRONMENT (auto-detect folder)
   $jdkPath = (Get-ChildItem "C:\Program Files\Microsoft\" -Filter "jdk-*" -Directory | Select-Object -First 1).FullName
   [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable("JAVA_HOME", $jdkPath, "User")
   $oldPath = [Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable("Path", "User")
   [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable("Path", "$jdkPath\bin;$oldPath", "User")

   → Close PowerShell. Open a NEW window.

3. VERIFY
   java -version
   javac -version
   where.exe java

4. CREATE FOLDER + FILE
   mkdir C:\Users\rkrev\javatest
   cd C:\Users\rkrev\javatest
   notepad MyFirstProgram.java

5. CODE
   public class MyFirstProgram {
       public static void main(String[] args) {
           System.out.println("Hello, this is my first Java program!");
       }
   }

6. SAVE (Ctrl+S) → close Notepad

7. CHECK FILE
   dir
   → must show MyFirstProgram.java (NOT .java.txt)

8. COMPILE
   javac MyFirstProgram.java
   → silent = success

9. RUN
   java MyFirstProgram
   → prints: Hello, this is my first Java program!


────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
KEY RULES
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
• cd "path" changes folders — bare path does NOT.
• Quote paths with spaces.
• Env vars apply only to NEW PowerShell windows.
• javac is silent on success.
• Class name = filename (case-sensitive).
• java X (no extension).
• Create .java via notepad, never by renaming .txt.
• Desktop on this PC is inside OneDrive.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
QUICK ERRORS
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
• "not recognized"        → PATH wrong / old window
• "file not found"        → wrong folder or .java.txt
• "should be declared in" → filename ≠ class name
• "Could not find main"   → compile first (javac)
• "Invalid filename:*"    → PowerShell ≠ wildcards
